=== P3 race demos

======================================================================
PART 1: The bug - two workers read the same PENDING transfer
======================================================================
  Worker 1 result: ('settled', 50035)
  Worker 2 result: ('error: Unbalanced ledger for transfer 50035: debits=2000.00, credits=1000.00', None)
  Per-transfer ledger entry counts:
    ('RACE-BUG-001', 2)
    ('RACE-BUG-002', 0)
  BUG: both workers targeted the same transfer_id. One worker either
  duplicates the settlement or fails with a confusing check_violation.

======================================================================
PART 2: UNIQUE constraint fix - settlement_claim rejects duplicates
======================================================================
  Worker 1 result: ('rejected: duplicate key value violates unique constraint "settlement_claim_transfer_id_key"', 50037)
  Worker 2 result: ('claimed', 50037)
  Claims recorded: [('W2', 50037)]
  FIX: exactly one worker holds the claim; the other is rejected cleanly.

======================================================================
PART 3: FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED - each worker gets a different transfer
======================================================================
  Worker 1 result: ('settled', 50039)
  Worker 2 result: ('settled', 50038)
  Per-transfer status / ledger counts:
    ('RACE-SKIP-001', 'SETTLED', 2)
    ('RACE-SKIP-002', 'SETTLED', 2)
    ('RACE-SKIP-003', 'PENDING', 0)
  FIX: each worker settled a different transfer; no duplicates.

======================================================================
PART 4: SERIALIZABLE isolation - expects SQLSTATE 40001
======================================================================
  Worker 1 result: ('40001 serialization_failure (40001)', None)
  Worker 2 result: ('settled', 50041)
  Under SERIALIZABLE isolation one worker commits and the other is
  aborted with SQLSTATE 40001 (serialization_failure) and must retry.

======================================================================
PART 5: READ COMMITTED vs REPEATABLE READ
======================================================================
  READ COMMITTED  reader saw: before=PENDING, after=SETTLED (sees the writer's commit)
  REPEATABLE READ reader saw: before=PENDING, after=PENDING (snapshot frozen at first read)

======================================================================
PART 6: Deadlock (40P01) and the fixed-account-order fix
======================================================================
  6a Buggy (lock in transfer direction):
    Worker 1: ('committed', 'RACE-DEAD-001')
    Worker 2: ('40P01 deadlock (DeadlockDetected)', 'RACE-DEAD-002')
    One worker should hit SQLSTATE 40P01 (deadlock_detected).
  6b Fixed (lock accounts in id order):
    Worker 1: ('committed', 'RACE-DEAD-001')
    Worker 2: ('committed', 'RACE-DEAD-002')
    Both workers commit; the lock-ordering prevents the deadlock.

